.Dd November 10, 2026
.Dt VFIO 4
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm vfio
.Nd PCI devices given to programs
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Pa /dev/vfio/vfio
.Pa /dev/vfio/N
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
is Linux's VFIO interface, API version 0, through which a program such as
QEMU drives a PCI function itself: its registers, its interrupts and its
DMA, which the IOMMU confines to the memory the program maps for it.
The x86-64 kernel has it; the i686 kernel has neither node.
A program uses it through the structures and ioctls of Linux's
.In linux/vfio.h .
.Pp
The IOMMU groups the functions it cannot tell apart, as Linux's
.Fn pci_device_group
does: a function shares the group of every function its path to the root
does not isolate from it by ACS, and of the other functions of its slot
when they have no ACS.
The Intel PCH root ports Linux takes as isolating are taken so here.
Groups are numbered from 0, unit by unit and each unit's functions in bus
order.
A function that no IOMMU unit translates is in no group, and with no unit
there is no group at all.
The boot records the count,
.Ql vfio: ready: G groups of F functions .
.Ss Binding
A function is given to the vfio-pci driver through
.Pa /sys ,
as on Linux:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
echo vfio-pci > /sys/bus/pci/devices/0000:00:02.0/driver_override
echo 0000:00:02.0 > /sys/bus/pci/drivers/vfio-pci/bind
.Ed
.Pp
or by writing its vendor and device to
.Pa /sys/bus/pci/drivers/vfio-pci/new_id ,
which binds every function of those ids that has no driver.
The bind fails with
.Er EBUSY
while a driver of the kernel's holds the function, and with
.Er EINVAL
for a bridge.
A group is viable while none of its functions but a bridge has a driver
other than vfio-pci.
.Xr sysfs 5
lists the files.
.Ss Nodes
.Pa /dev/vfio/vfio ,
character device 10:196, mode 0666, opens a new container.
.Pa /dev/vfio/N ,
character device 242:N, mode 0600, is group N; one descriptor may hold it
at a time, and a second open fails with
.Er EBUSY .
Every descriptor
.Nm
gives is open for reading and writing.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It a container
.Dv VFIO_GET_API_VERSION ,
which answers 0;
.Dv VFIO_CHECK_EXTENSION ,
1 for
.Dv VFIO_TYPE1_IOMMU ,
.Dv VFIO_TYPE1v2_IOMMU
and
.Dv VFIO_UNMAP_ALL
and 0 for the rest;
.Dv VFIO_SET_IOMMU ;
and, once it is set,
.Dv VFIO_IOMMU_GET_INFO ,
.Dv VFIO_IOMMU_MAP_DMA
and
.Dv VFIO_IOMMU_UNMAP_DMA .
.It a group
.Dv VFIO_GROUP_GET_STATUS ,
.Dv VFIO_GROUP_SET_CONTAINER ,
which fails with
.Er EPERM
for a group that is not viable,
.Dv VFIO_GROUP_UNSET_CONTAINER ,
which fails with
.Er EBUSY
while a device of the group is open, and
.Dv VFIO_GROUP_GET_DEVICE_FD ,
which takes a function's name,
.Ql 0000:BB:DD.F ,
bound to vfio-pci and in the group, once the container has its IOMMU.
.It a device
.Dv VFIO_DEVICE_GET_INFO ,
.Dv VFIO_DEVICE_GET_REGION_INFO ,
.Dv VFIO_DEVICE_GET_IRQ_INFO ,
.Dv VFIO_DEVICE_SET_IRQS
and
.Dv VFIO_DEVICE_RESET .
.Dv VFIO_DEVICE_GET_PCI_HOT_RESET_INFO
and
.Dv VFIO_DEVICE_PCI_HOT_RESET
fail with
.Er ENODEV ,
as on a root bus.
.El
.Ss Type1
.Dv VFIO_SET_IOMMU
gives every function of the container's groups one IOMMU domain.
It fails with
.Er EPERM
on a host whose interrupts are not remapped, where a function could raise
any interrupt by a message, unless the boot line has
.Ql vfio_iommu_type1.allow_unsafe_interrupts=1 ,
as Linux's module parameter.
.Dv VFIO_IOMMU_GET_INFO
gives page sizes of 4 KiB, 2 MiB and 1 GiB, the valid IOVA ranges, which
leave out each function's reserved regions but the relaxable ones, and
the count of mappings left.
.Pp
.Dv VFIO_IOMMU_MAP_DMA
pins the caller's RAM pages, charged against its
.Dv RLIMIT_MEMLOCK
as
.Xr limits 7
says, and maps them at the IOVA with the access its flags give.
It also follows mappings of device memory, including PCI BARs, without
charging them as locked RAM.
Its addresses and size are multiples of 4 KiB, and its range lies within
one valid range, or it fails with
.Er EINVAL ;
it fails with
.Er EEXIST
over a mapping, and with
.Er ENOSPC
at 65535 mappings.
A mapping with
.Dv VFIO_DMA_MAP_FLAG_WRITE
of a writable shared mapping of a file on a disk fails with
.Er EFAULT ,
as on Linux, since the file's write-back would never see the device's
stores.
When the program unmaps the pinned memory the device can no longer reach
those pages.
When its address space ends, every mapping, of RAM or of device pages,
stays with its pages until its unmap or until the last group leaves the
container, as on Linux.
While the address space lives, a mapping containing device pages also
loses its DMA access before a protection change or the device revokes its
pages; after its end, neither is heard.
.Dv VFIO_IOMMU_UNMAP_DMA
removes the mappings of a range, or every one with
.Dv VFIO_DMA_UNMAP_FLAG_ALL ;
under
.Dv VFIO_TYPE1v2_IOMMU
a range that would split a mapping fails with
.Er EINVAL .
.Ss Regions
A device's region N lies at offset N << 40 of its descriptor and is read
and written with
.Xr pread 2
and
.Xr pwrite 2 :
the six BARs, 0 to 5, the ROM, 6, and the configuration space, 7.
An Intel display adds, read-only and each with Linux's type capability,
its OpRegion, 9, and the configuration spaces of the host bridge, 10, and
the LPC bridge, 11.
A memory BAR of a page or more can be mapped with
.Xr mmap 2 ,
uncached; its pages fault in only while the function decodes memory, and
a reset or turning decoding off takes them from every mapping.
The MSI-X table's bytes read as all ones and take no write.
.Pp
The configuration space is the function's but for what
.Nm
keeps, as on Linux: the BARs and the ROM's address, the Interrupt Disable
bit, the interrupt line, the MSI capability and the MSI-X enable and mask
bits.
A write cannot start a function level reset, and the OpRegion's address
takes no write.
The extended space reads as the function answers it and takes no write.
.Ss Interrupts
.Dv VFIO_DEVICE_SET_IRQS
gives an eventfd to the line, to MSI, to each MSI-X entry, and to the
error and request indexes.
At an interrupt of the line,
.Nm
masks it by the function's Interrupt Disable bit and signals its eventfd;
an unmask, by ioctl or by an eventfd given to
.Dv VFIO_IRQ_SET_ACTION_UNMASK ,
lifts the mask.
The line is offered only to a function that masks it that way.
MSI-X entries can be set and cleared while MSI-X is on.
.Ss Release
When the last descriptor and mapping of a device close, or their owner
dies, every interrupt goes off, bus mastering goes off, the function is
reset and its state at the open written back.
It stays bound to vfio-pci.
.Sh LIMITS
MSI of more than one message is answered with one, as Linux answers when
it gives fewer; MSI-X has every entry.
There is no VGA region, no hot reset, and no
.Dv VFIO_DEVICE_IOEVENTFD
or
.Dv VFIO_DEVICE_FEATURE ,
which fail with
.Er ENOTTY ,
so no migration.
The error and request eventfds are never signalled.
A device's descriptor answers
.Xr lseek 2 ,
where Linux's is not seekable.
At most 64 groups, 128 functions in groups and 16 ids given by
.Pa new_id .
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width /sys/kernel/iommu_groups
.It Pa /dev/vfio/vfio
The container's node.
.It Pa /dev/vfio/N
Group N's node.
.It Pa /sys/kernel/iommu_groups
The groups, their functions and their reserved regions.
.It Pa /sys/bus/pci/drivers/vfio-pci
The driver's bind, unbind, new_id and remove_id.
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ readlink /sys/bus/pci/devices/0000:00:02.0/iommu_group
\&../../../kernel/iommu_groups/0
.Ed
.Pp
The native case
.Nm vfiomusl
checks the container, each group's node and its entries of
.Pa /sys .
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr kvm 4 ,
.Xr sysfs 5 ,
.Xr limits 7
